/-!
# q-expansion principle at level one: invariance implies f∈ℚ[j]

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.mem_adjoin_jq_of_hasSum_of_slash_invariant`
(`8dc4e43d-6a5b-5cf0-b976-4b85a75a17e2`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `6cd85d0b-d0bb-5e15-b57d-f5d694c67ccd`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $f$ be a formal Laurent series with rational coefficients, i.e. an element of `LaurentSeries ℚ`
$=$ `HahnSeries ℤ ℚ`, and let $F \colon \mathfrak H \to \mathbb C$ be a function on the upper
half-plane. Assume that for every $\tau \in \mathfrak H$ the family indexed by $m \in \mathbb Z$
whose $m$-th term is the complex number $f_m \, q^m$, where $f_m$ is the coefficient of $f$ in
degree $m$ viewed in $\mathbb C$ and $q =$ `Function.Periodic.qParam 1 τ` $= e^{2\pi i \tau}$, is
summable with sum $F(\tau)$; and assume that $F(\gamma \cdot \tau) = F(\tau)$ for every $\gamma \in
\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ and every $\tau \in \mathfrak H$, for the usual action of
`Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup (Fin 2) ℤ` on the upper half-plane. Then $f$ belongs to the $\mathbb
Q$-subalgebra `Algebra.adjoin ℚ {ModularCurve.jq}` of $\mathbb Q((q))$ generated by the single
element [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157), which is by definition the product of
the Hahn series $q^{-1}$ with the image in $\mathbb Q((q))$ of the integral power series `jNum`
under $\mathbb Z \to \mathbb Q$, that is, the formal $q$-expansion of $E_4^3/\Delta$. In other
words, $f = P(\mathtt{jq})$ for some polynomial $P$ over $\mathbb Q$. No bound on the pole order of
$f$, no holomorphy of $F$ and no condition at the cusp are assumed.

This is the $q$-expansion principle at level one in the form: a rational formal Laurent series that
converges on $\mathfrak H$ to an $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$-invariant function is a polynomial in
$j$ with rational coefficients, the $q$-series counterpart of the classical statement that the field
of level-one modular functions holomorphic on $\mathfrak H$ is generated by $j$. It is used in the
treatment of modular curves of level $N$, for instance to identify $q$-expansions of
$\Gamma_0(N)$-invariant functions and in the analysis of the Fricke involution and of products of
modular units.

